
  AutoHelix v0.1.1
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Goal       Make `kernel.neff` execute faster on the device without changing
             what it computes. `kernel.neff` is the compiled NEFF of the nkilib
             GDN kernel `nkilib.experimental.gdn.gdn_cte.gdn_cte` -- chunked
             prefill (chunked gated delta-rule) -- for batch_size 32, seq_len
             128, head_dim 128, dtype bfloat16, on one logical NeuronCore at
             LNC=1 (one physical core), one rank. The unedited NEFF and its
             measurements are in `baseline/`; measure the current one yourself
             rather than trusting any number written down here. THE ONLY FILE
             YOU MAY CHANGE IS `kernel.neff`. There is no kernel source in this
             repo and recompiling one is not the task: the work is to edit the
             compiled binary. Every other file (tools/, bench/, baseline/) is
             reverted before your iteration is judged. ## How to edit a NEFF
             Everything you need is documented in
             `/home/ubuntu/workspace/neff_doc` -- read it, do not guess. Start
             with its `README.md`, then: chapter 14 optimization playbook: how
             to find where the time goes chapter 13 editing guide: the
             modify/run/check/measure procedure and a worked example chapter 10
             synchronisation: the semaphore model and the rules an edit must
             respect chapter 6 the 64-byte instruction slot; chapter 7 what each
             instruction's fields mean chapter 9 DMA (queued and DGE); chapter 5
             SBUF/PSUM addressing chapter 4 the definition file and the variable
             table chapter 15 the tools, with what each one is for
             `neff_doc/experiments/` holds two finished runs of this same loop
             on other GDN kernels, with their per-iteration notes: what worked,
             what was refuted, and the measurement that closed it. Those kernels
             are not this one, so check any claim against your own profile
             before relying on it. The tools live in
             `/home/ubuntu/workspace/neff_doc/tools/`. Use them; they are
             tested: neff.py unpack kernel.neff work/ unpack
             (payload/sgNN/<Engine>0.bin are the streams) isa.py
             work/payload/sg00/DVE0.bin decode every instruction slot, by vendor
             field name semaphores.py work sg00 check no wait can exceed its
             supply (catches hangs) neff.py pack work/ kernel.neff repack
             (recomputes the header size and both MD5s) neff_diff.py
             baseline/kernel.neff kernel.neff see exactly which bytes/fields you
             changed Run them with `/home/ubuntu/workspace/.venv/bin/python`. ##
             The loop's rules * `tools/verify.py` must pass: the NEFF runs on
             the device with fixed inputs and every output must stay within the
             `max_abs_err_limit` in `bench/manifest.json` of the torch golden.
             The unedited NEFF already spends most of that budget
             (`baseline/verify_baseline.json`; the verifier prints how much of
             the limit each output uses), so an edit that changes the arithmetic
             will fail. * `tools/launch_rules.py` must pass: still exactly 1
             subgraph(s) (`sg00`), no collectives, no replica groups, `num_tpb
             == 1`, same inputs and output heads. Using more cores or more ranks
             than the baseline would lower the metric by spending more hardware
             rather than by making the kernel better, so it is rejected. * The
             metric is `total_exec_time_us` from `tools/profile.py` (median of 5
             captures) and LOWER IS BETTER. An iteration that regresses it by
             more than 2% is rejected. ## Finding where the time goes Measure
             the NEFF you are holding; do not work from remembered numbers, they
             go stale as the loop changes the NEFF. `tools/profile.py` prints
             the per-engine busy time and writes `profile_latest.json`;
             `baseline/profile_baseline.json` is the unedited NEFF for
             comparison. `tools/profile.py --parquet prof` ingests the profile
             into `prof/Instruction.parquet` (`engine`, `pcore_idx`,
             `compiler_pc`, `opcode`, `duration_ns`, `start_ts`, `end_ts`) so
             you can find the slots that actually cost time, and chapter 11 maps
             a slot back to its NKI line. What the numbers mean for this kernel,
             and what is worth attacking, is yours to work out from the
             measurement -- the notes and the review carry what earlier
             iterations already settled. This kernel runs in about 1351 us.
             check the spread `tools/profile.py` reports before believing a
             small win. ## Working method 1. Measure or read the current profile
             before changing anything. 2. Form one hypothesis, name the slots it
             touches, and predict the effect. 3. Make the smallest edit that
             tests it. Check `semaphores.py`, then `verify.py`, then
             `profile.py`. 4. Write what you learned in `notes/` -- iterations
             are isolated and `notes/` is the only thing that survives,
             including after a rejected iteration. Record failures too: a lever
             that turns out to be closed is worth as much as a win. 5. If an
             edit is wrong, `cp baseline/kernel.neff kernel.neff` and start
             again. Do not claim a win you cannot separate from the run-to-run
             spread; repeat the measurement instead.
